// PROBLEM BANK: WEEK 20 - REAL-TIME SYSTEMS (100 Problems)

===== CATEGORY 20.1: GC METRICS & MEASUREMENT (30 Problems) =====

[20.1.1] Measure GC pause time - Track pause duration when collection triggers
[20.1.2] Track heap statistics - Monitor used/total memory before/after GC
[20.1.3] Monitor allocation rate - bytes/sec and predict GC triggers
[20.1.4] Measure collection frequency - How often does GC run?
[20.1.5] Latency histogram - Bucket pauses: 0-1ms, 1-10ms, 10-100ms, 100ms+
[20.1.6] Correlation: allocation vs GC - Heavy allocation causes pauses?
[20.1.7] Request latency with GC - Measure added latency when GC occurs
[20.1.8] Memory fragmentation - Track free blocks, largest contiguous
[20.1.9] Generational efficiency - Track young object lifetime vs pause
[20.1.10] Full collection cost - Time and frequency of major collections

[20.1.11] Measure pause variability - std deviation of pause times
[20.1.12] Identify pause outliers - Detect GC spikes vs normal pauses
[20.1.13] Predict next pause - Use history to forecast GC event
[20.1.14] Pause impact on throughput - Miss deadline when GC happens?
[20.1.15] Maximum pause tracking - P99.9 worst case
[20.1.16] Pause frequency - GC every 10s? every 100s?
[20.1.17] Heap size vs pause - Does bigger heap = longer pauses?
[20.1.18] Collection efficiency - pause_time / bytes_reclaimed
[20.1.19] Young vs full pause - Compare minor/major collection times
[20.1.20] Pause budget tracking - Enforce max pause per second

[20.1.21] Define real-time constraint - Deadline < X microseconds
[20.1.22] Deadline success rate - % of requests meeting deadline
[20.1.23] Model GC incompatibility - Show why GC breaks hard real-time
[20.1.24] Worst-case latency - Design for max possible pause
[20.1.25] Jitter budget - Allowed p99-p50 deviation
[20.1.26] Deadline miss analysis - Which requests fail? correlation with GC?
[20.1.27] Safety margin - Deadline 500µs, process in 400µs = 100µs buffer
[20.1.28] Admission control - Can we process this request in time?
[20.1.29] Graceful degradation - Drop low priority under load
[20.1.30] End-to-end latency breakdown - Receive + validate + process + send

===== CATEGORY 20.2: POOLING & ALLOCATION (30 Problems) =====

[20.2.1] Fixed-size object pool - Pre-allocate 100 items, reuse (10 problems)
[20.2.2-20.2.10] Pool variants - dynamic sizing, bounded, factory functions

[20.2.11] Arena allocator - Single 1MB block, bump pointer allocation
[20.2.12] Request-scoped arena - Create per-request, free at end
[20.2.13] Arena statistics - Track usage, fragmentation, peak
[20.2.14] Nested pools - Pool of vectors with capacity
[20.2.15] Pool reuse metrics - Track allocation savings

[20.2.16] Cache-line aligned structs - Prevent false sharing
[20.2.17] Memory layout optimization - Field ordering for cache
[20.2.18] SIMD-friendly layout - Aligned arrays for vectorization
[20.2.19] Prefetching patterns - Improve cache hits
[20.2.20] Page-aligned buffers - Pre-fault pages to avoid page faults

[20.2.21] String pool - Deduplicate strings
[20.2.22] Number pool - Pre-allocated numbers for arithmetic
[20.2.23] Buffer pool - Reusable byte buffers
[20.2.24] Connection pool - Pre-created sockets
[20.2.25] Thread pool integration - Use pools with actor pool from Week 19

[20.2.26] Stack allocation - Use stack not heap
[20.2.27] Static allocation - Compile-time constants
[20.2.28] Custom allocators - Implement simple allocator
[20.2.29] Zero-allocation patterns - No new allocations in hot path
[20.2.30] Allocation budgeting - Track bytes allocated per request

===== CATEGORY 20.3: LATENCY ANALYSIS (25 Problems) =====

[20.3.1-20.3.10] Measure end-to-end latency breakdown (10 problems)
[20.3.11-20.3.20] Percentile calculation (p50, p95, p99, p99.9) - 10 problems
[20.3.21-20.3.25] Jitter measurement & analysis - 5 problems

===== CATEGORY 20.4: JITTER ELIMINATION (15 Problems) =====

[20.4.1] Identify jitter sources - GC vs locks vs cache
[20.4.2] Cache optimization - Align data for locality
[20.4.3] CPU affinity - Pin threads to cores
[20.4.4] Disable frequency scaling - Fixed CPU frequency
[20.4.5] NUMA awareness - Keep data on same node
[20.4.6] Lock-free operations - Atomics instead of mutex
[20.4.7] Predictable memory allocation - Pre-allocate everything
[20.4.8] Avoid context switches - Use actor model not threads
[20.4.9] Warm-up runs - Prime caches before measurement
[20.4.10] Statistical filtering - Remove outliers from measurement
[20.4.11] Latency SLA - Verify 99% meet deadline
[20.4.12] Outlier compensation - What if we must accept some jitter?
[20.4.13] Deadline guarantees - Proof that design meets deadline
[20.4.14] Graceful degradation - Lower latency p50 when p99 threatened
[20.4.15] Real-time quality - Measure actual system (not benchmark)

